NAME
       lvm - the logical volume manager and its command shell

SYNOPSIS
       lvm [COMMAND [ARGUMENTS...]]

DESCRIPTION
       LVM (the logical volume manager) builds flexible storage out of disks
       and partitions in three layers:

       Physical volumes (PVs)
              Disks or partitions prepared with pvcreate. Each is cut into
              equal pieces called physical extents (4 MiB by default).

       Volume groups (VGs)
              Pools made of one or more PVs (vgcreate, vgextend). Their size
              is the total of their PVs.

       Logical volumes (LVs)
              Parts of a VG used like partitions (lvcreate). They hold file
              systems or swap, appear as /dev/VG/LV and /dev/mapper/VG-LV,
              and can grow later (lvextend) while in use.

       Every LVM task has its own command (pvcreate, vgcreate, lvcreate,
       lvs...). "lvm COMMAND ARGS" runs the same commands; plain "lvm" opens
       an lvm> prompt that accepts them one per line, which is handy in the
       rd.break emergency shell, where only the lvm binary may be present.

       The usual order for new storage: partition the disk (or use it
       whole), pvcreate, vgcreate (or vgextend an existing VG), lvcreate,
       mkfs, mkdir the mount point, a line in /etc/fstab, mount -a.

COMMANDS
       Physical volumes: pvcreate, pvremove, pvs, pvdisplay, pvscan,
       pvresize, pvmove, pvchange.
       Volume groups: vgcreate, vgextend, vgreduce, vgremove, vgrename, vgs,
       vgdisplay, vgscan, vgchange.
       Logical volumes: lvcreate, lvextend, lvreduce, lvresize, lvremove,
       lvrename, lvs, lvdisplay, lvscan, lvchange.

EXAMPLES
       From the emergency shell, list and activate the volumes:

           lvm lvs
           lvm vgchange -ay

FILES
       /etc/lvm/lvm.conf
              Settings for every LVM command.

SEE ALSO
       pvcreate(8), vgcreate(8), lvcreate(8), lvs(8), lvextend(8)
